course: everyday
lesson: 97
title: Moving, new schools, change and grief
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Big changes, like moving house, starting a new school or a new year group, feel strange at first because everything is unfamiliar; knowing what to expect, keeping usual routines and giving yourself time make them feel normal again.
explain: When a pet or a person we love dies, we feel grief, a mix of sadness, missing them, sometimes anger or guilt; grief is the price of love, and it is normal and real, whether for a person or a pet.
explain: Death means the body has stopped working forever, so the person or animal cannot come back; it is not like sleep, and thoughts or wishes can never make someone die.
explain: Grief comes and goes in waves: you may cry one moment and laugh the next, and laughing does not mean you have forgotten; talking, crying and keeping memories all help.
explain: A boy in Colorado whose dog has died makes a drawing of the dog chasing snow, puts it on the fridge, and when he misses her he looks at it and tells his mum one of his favourite memories.
fact: Asking questions and looking at pictures of a new place makes moving less scary.
fact: Unpacking your favourite things first helps a new bedroom feel like home.
fact: Many schools have buddies to help new pupils find their way.
fact: Welcoming a new pupil is how you would want to be welcomed yourself.
fact: Change feels strange at first but soon becomes normal, and routines help you feel steady.
fact: Grief is normal, and talking, crying and keeping memories help.
fact: Sadness about a pet is real, because pets are family members for many people.
fact: A new pet does not replace the old one, and it is wise to wait and talk it over first.
fact: Death means the body has stopped working forever; it is not sleep, and the person cannot come back.
fact: Thoughts and wishes cannot make anyone die.
fact: At a funeral it is fine to cry or to be quiet, and asking what will happen beforehand helps.
fact: A funeral is when family and friends come together to say goodbye to someone who has died.
fact: Telling a young child that someone «went to sleep» can confuse them and make them scared of sleep, so clear words like «died» are kinder.
fact: Laughing and having fun soon after someone died is fine; grief comes and goes.
fact: When a friend's family member dies, a simple «I'm sorry» and being there means more than perfect words.
exercise: Your family tells you that you are moving to another city. What helps most? | Ask questions, look at pictures of the new place and say proper goodbyes | Refuse to talk about it until the day you move | Pretend it is not happening for as long as possible, then hope it gets cancelled | Leave without telling your friends you are going
exercise: Your new bedroom feels strange. What can make it feel like home? | Unpack your favourite things first and arrange them your way | Keep everything packed in boxes for now | Sleep in your parents' room until you are grown | Paint the walls without asking anyone first
exercise: It is your first day at a new school and you do not know where anything is. What should you do? | Ask a teacher or a buddy to show you | Wander around alone until you find it | Stay in one classroom for the whole day | Go home because you are completely lost
exercise: A new pupil joins your class. What is kind? | Say hello, show them around and invite them to play | Wait to see if anyone else talks to them first | Ask them lots of questions in front of everyone | Leave them alone until they make friends
exercise: You are moving up to a new year group with a new teacher. What should you expect? | The first week may feel strange, but it soon becomes normal | The whole year will feel as strange as the first day | Nothing will feel different at all, because every year is exactly the same | A new teacher means you will be unhappy all year
exercise: Something in your life changes suddenly. What helps you feel steady? | Talking about it and keeping your usual routines | Changing everything else as well, all at once | Staying in your room until it all feels normal | Never mentioning the change to anyone at all
exercise: Your hamster dies. What helps? | Cry if you need, talk about memories, perhaps make a drawing | Pretend you never had a hamster | Get rid of every picture of the hamster | Tell yourself that crying over a pet is silly
exercise: Someone says, «It was only a pet.» What is true? | Your sadness is real, because pets are family to many people | They are right, so you should not feel sad | Only dogs are worth being sad about | Pets do not really feel love, so it does not matter
exercise: Your cat has just died and you ask for a new kitten tomorrow. What is wise? | Wait a while and talk it through with your family | Get the new kitten straight away to stop the sadness | Choose a kitten that looks exactly the same | Never have any pet again in your life
exercise: Your grandmother dies. What helps you with grief? | Ask questions, cry, talk about her and keep a memory box | Keep busy and never talk about her again | Hide your sadness from everyone in the family | Throw away anything that reminds you of her
exercise: A young cousin asks if Grandad is just asleep. What is the true answer? | No, his body has stopped working forever and he cannot come back | Yes, he is only asleep and he may wake up one day if we wait | He has gone away on a long holiday | He will come back if we are very good
exercise: Why is it better to use clear words like «died» with children? | Clear words help them understand, and they will not fear sleep | Clear words are more polite at a funeral | Children never understand kind words | It stops them from feeling sad
exercise: A girl was cross with her aunt and wished she would go away. Then her aunt died. What is true? | Thoughts and wishes cannot make someone die | Her angry wish must have caused her aunt's death | She must say sorry to everyone at the funeral | She should never be cross with anyone ever again
exercise: You are going to your first funeral. What is good to know? | You can ask what will happen, and it is fine to cry or be quiet | You must not cry at all, because crying upsets the family of the person | Children must stay silent and not ask anything | You should only go if you knew the person well
exercise: A boy laughs at a funny film a week after his grandad died, then feels bad. What is true? | It is fine; grief comes and goes, and he has not forgotten | He should feel bad, he ought to still be crying | Laughing shows he did not really love his grandad | He should not watch films for a whole year
exercise: Your friend's mum has died. What can you do? | Say «I'm sorry» and be there for your friend | Avoid your friend because you do not know what to say | Ask lots of questions about how she died | Tell your friend she will feel better soon
test: A girl in Phoenix is moving to Alaska. Which plan will help most? | Look at photos of her new town and say goodbye to friends properly | Not think about it until the van arrives | Hide in her room on moving day | Refuse to unpack until she likes the new house
test: A new boy from Ireland joins a class in Ohio and looks lost. Which pupil does the right thing? | One who says hello and shows him where things are | One who waits for the teacher to deal with the new boy | One who asks about his accent in front of everyone | One who decides he probably just wants to be alone
test: Why do routines help after a big change? | Familiar things make you feel steady and safe | Routines stop the change from happening | Routines make you forget the change | Routines are only meant for very young children who cannot cope
test: Which is true about grief? | It is the price of love, and it comes and goes | It always lasts exactly one month | It only happens when a person dies | It means you should stay sad all of the time, or you did not care
test: A boy's tortoise dies and his uncle says, «Just get another one.» How might the boy feel, and why? | Still sad, because a new pet cannot replace the one he loved | Better, because one tortoise is just the same as any other tortoise | Happy, because he wanted a dog anyway | Nothing, because pets are just animals
test: A five-year-old thinks her grandma died because she was naughty. What should an older brother tell her? | Nothing she did or thought could make Grandma die | She should be extra good from now on so that it does not happen again | It is a secret and they should not talk about it | Grandma is only sleeping and will wake up
test: Your best friend's grandad has died and he is back at school. Which is the kindest thing to say? | I'm really sorry about your grandad | Well, at least he was very old, wasn't he | I know exactly how you feel right now | You should try your best not to be sad
test: Why do people hold funerals? | So people can say goodbye together | To decide who gets the person's things | So the person can hear them one last time | Because crying is only allowed there
test: A girl worries that going to sleep could make her die like her grandad. What is the truth? | Sleep is safe; death is the body stopping forever, not sleep | Sleeping too long can make your body stop and die, so it is risky | Only old people die in their sleep | She should stay awake to be safe
test: we moving to Perth, never been, scared | Ask questions and look at pictures of the new place | Refuse to go and stay behind | Try not to think about it | Pack nothing at all and hope the move gets cancelled
test: new house, what box do i unpack first | Your favourite things, so it feels like home | The kitchen pots and pans | The boxes of winter coats | Nothing at all until the new room has been painted
test: i wished he'd go away n then grandpa died, its my fault | No, thoughts and wishes cannot make anyone die | Yes, wishes can make bad things happen to people | Yes, so you must never wish anything bad ever again | Maybe, if you wished it very hard and very often
test: laughed at a joke a week after nanna died, is that bad | No, grief comes and goes, fun is fine | Yes, you must stay sad for a whole year | Yes, laughing means you did not love her | Yes, say sorry to the whole family for laughing
explain: A boy in Leeds whose dog died was told by a friend not to cry; he held it in for weeks and got stomach aches and bad dreams until he talked with his mum and cried.
explain: A girl in Texas moved to a new town and did not tell anyone at the new school she was struggling; she ate lunch alone for months until a teacher noticed and set her up with a buddy.
fact: Crying and talking about a loss are normal and help people heal.
fact: Children who move school settle in faster when they tell a teacher or parent if they are finding it hard.
test: my dog died, mate says crying is for babies | Crying is normal and helps, talk to someone you trust | He's right, never cry | Hide your feelings forever | Get a new dog the same day and just forget about the old one
test: new school, eating lunch alone every day | Tell a teacher or parent, they can help you make friends | Keep quiet until you leave | Pretend you like being alone, then nobody will notice you | Stop going to lunch
test: grandad died n i can't stop thinking about it | Talk to someone you trust, grief gets easier with time | Never talk about him again | Feeling this sad means something is wrong with you inside | Only grown-ups feel grief
test: did u hear, Mia's dog died n she cried in class, Jake reckons crying over a dog is pathetic lol. what's the real lesson | Grief over a pet is real and crying is normal, not pathetic | Jake is right that crying over a dog in front of the class is pathetic | Mia should hide her sadness until she gets home | Only crying over people counts as real grief
test: kids at the bus stop say the new girl got expelled from her old school n that's why she moved, someone heard it from someone. what's probably true | It is just a rumour; families move for many ordinary reasons | It must be true, since so many kids are saying it | It is true, because new pupils who move in the middle of a year always have a secret | It is true if the person who heard it is older
test: my cousin said Sam's grandad died n Sam was laughing at lunch next day, so he obviously didn't care. should i pass that on | No, grief comes and goes, and passing it on would hurt Sam | Yes, everyone should know Sam did not care | Yes, but only to Sam's closest friends | Yes, because laughing so soon proves he did not really love his grandad
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a child newly arrived at an unfamiliar school ought to make known to a teacher any difficulty in settling. | Pupils who disclose such difficulty settle in more rapidly | Teachers are obliged to transfer all such pupils elsewhere at once | Disclosure exempts the pupil from lessons for a period | Silence is preferred by schools in all such matters
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises death, as it ought to be explained to a young child? | The body has ceased to function permanently and cannot resume | The deceased is in a prolonged sleep and may awaken | The deceased has departed upon an extended journey | The condition may be reversed by sufficiently good conduct on the part of the family
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the thoughts and wishes of a child in respect of a subsequent death. | Thoughts and wishes are incapable of causing any death | A sufficiently intense wish may occasion the death of another | Angry thoughts render the child partly responsible for it | Wishes are capable of hastening an illness in another person
exam: It is given that a pupil laughs at an amusing film one week subsequent to the death of a grandparent. Determine which statement is correct. | Such conduct is normal, grief occurring in alternating waves | Such conduct demonstrates an absence of genuine affection | Such conduct ought to be prohibited for a period of at least one year | Such conduct signifies that mourning has been concluded
